function rep(a, b) { const i = s.indexOf(a); if (i < 0 || s.indexOf(a, i + 1) >= 0) throw new Error('anchor ' + a.slice(0, 80)); s = s.replace(a, b); }
rep('The total here is 36.', 'The total here is 35.');
rep('| `calibration` | 2 | Calibration tests. |', '| `calibration` | 1 | Calibration tests, each a project of its own. |');
rep(`| \`calibration_list\` | R | Tests for the current printer and filament with parameters, safe ranges, automatic-measurement support, and machine-stored results where firmware reports them. | \`CalibUtils\`; \`MachineObject\` calibration queries | 16 tests, 32 results |
| \`calibration_generate\` | M | Generate one test's geometry and configuration onto a new plate. Analysis is reasoning over a photo or camera frame; applying a result uses \`settings_apply_patch\` on the filament scope with \`persistAs\`. | \`CalibUtils\` generators | |
`, `| \`calibration_generate\` | M | Implemented, in-app and over MCP. Replace the open project with one of OrcaSlicer's calibration tests (temperature, flow, pressure advance) as a project of its own: Orca's generators call \`new_project()\` and edit the project-wide presets, so a test cannot be a plate of another project. The open project is first released through \`project_open\`'s path (saved to its version history, unsaved preset edits dropped); a range Orca's dialog refuses is refused before anything closes. Returns the plate, its objects and the range applied, read back from the plate's print, and a summary sentence; for the in-app Agent the test's project opens its chat with it. The per-layer steps are runtime-only in Orca, so each plate reports them as \`calibration\` while its print holds them. Analysis is reasoning over a photo; a result is saved with \`settings_apply_patch\` on the filament scope with \`persistAs\`, a named copy no other project is switched to. | \`Plater::calib_temp\`, \`calib_flowrate\`, \`calib_pa\` | 64 objects |

\`calibration_list\` was dropped (Kenneth, 2026-10-21): which test fits, its ranges and how to read it are static knowledge in the \`calibration\` skill, and the workspace already reports the printer, filament and nozzle. Bambu's automatic flow-dynamics calibration, whose results stay on the printer, is not a tool.
`);
rep('3. The user reports stringing on a Bambu printer. List the tests, generate a temperature tower, read the user\'s photo, propose a filament temperature, then preview it and apply it with `persistAs`. Needs `calibration` and the filament scope.',
    '3. The user reports stringing on a Bambu printer. Judge that a temperature tower is worth it, say that the open project will be saved and closed before generating it, read the user\'s photo, propose a filament temperature, then preview it and apply it with `persistAs` under the calibrated-copy name. Needs `calibration`, the `calibration` skill and the filament scope.');
fs.writeFileSync(p, crlf ? s.replace(/\n/g, '\r\n') : s);
console.log('ok');

