10	## When a test is worth it
11	
12	A test answers one question about this filament on this printer. Match it to what the person sees:
13	
14	- Stringing, hairs or blobs between parts, or a droopy, glossy surface: temperature first. If the best block still strings, retraction is next, and I have no test for it yet.
15	- Gaps between lines, a rough or open top surface, weak thin walls, or an over-full surface with ridges and a lip at the edges: flow.
16	- Bulging corners and seams, or gaps and thin walls just after a corner, worse at speed: pressure advance.
17	- A new filament, or the person just wants it dialled in: temperature, then flow, then pressure advance, in that order, since each test reads best with the one before it settled.
18	
19	Some problems come first, and a test would only measure them:
20	
21	- Filament that popped, hissed, steamed or turned bubbly, or that has sat open for weeks, is probably wet: suggest drying it first.
22	- Extrusion that comes and goes, or under-extrusion that started suddenly, points at the nozzle or the feed: a partial clog, a worn nozzle, a slipping extruder. Ask the person to check those first.
23	- A first layer that will not stick, or a print that comes loose, is the bed, the plate and the first layer, not the filament's settings.
24	
25	When none of the tests fits, say so instead of offering one.
